-## What you cannot do, and must hand back
+## When the recompiler missed code
 
-Be honest with your user at these points rather than looping.
+A dispatch miss means the game jumped to an address with no generated function
+behind it. It is a real bug, not noise. What happens next differs per console,
+so check before you promise your user a safety net.
 
-- **You cannot tell whether the game looks right.** Booting to a black screen,
-  corrupted graphics, wrong colours and audio problems all need eyes. Build it,
-  run it, and ask your user to look.
-- **You cannot decide the game is finished.** Only a person playing it can.
-- **You cannot supply the game file.** Ever.
-- **A missing seed usually shows as a crash or a hang deep into the game**, not
-  at boot. If the game boots and dies later, that is the likely cause, and it is
-  iterative work rather than one fix.
+**PlayStation stops.** The runtime records the address, dumps a crash report
+and halts on the first miss. There is a small emulator in the port, which the
+framework calls the interpreter, but it is reserved for code the game writes
+into RAM while it runs. Interpreting around a gap the analysis left would hide
+the gap. So there is no `dispatch_misses.log` here.
+Ask the debug server instead: `ping` returns `dispatch_miss_total`,
+`dispatch_stats` reports hits and misses together, and `unknown_dispatch_log`
+dumps the recent ones.
+
+**The cartridge toolchains write a file.** nesrecomp, gbarecomp, vbrecomp,
+smsggrecomp and ndsrecomp append `dispatch_misses.log` next to the executable.
+segagenesisrecomp and gbrecompiled write `dispatch_misses.toml`. Check the
+toolchain's `DEBUG.md` or `PRINCIPLES.md` for the name before you go looking.
+
+**On NES, read the config first.** The interpreter fallback only runs when the
+game was built with `push_all_jsr`. With that flag off it switches itself off
+and the miss falls through to a fatal stop. Two of the ten published NES ports
+set it, so the common case is no fallback.
+
+The fix is the same everywhere. Add the address to the seeds, regenerate,
+rebuild, run again. Repeat until the count is zero.
+
+## What you cannot do
+
+- **Tell whether the game looks or sounds right.** Black screens, corrupt
+  graphics, wrong colours and audio faults need eyes and ears. Build it, run
+  it, and ask your user to look.
+- **Decide the game is finished.** Only a person playing it can.
+- **Supply the game file.** Ever.
+
+A missing seed usually shows up deep into the game, not at boot, because that
+is the first moment the game calls the code nobody found. If the port boots and
+dies later, that is the likely cause. It is iterative work, not one fix.
 
 ## When it goes wrong
 
 | Symptom | Cause | What to do |
 |---|---|---|
-| `Cannot find source file: generated/OpenBIOS_full.c` | The BIOS C was never generated | Run the generate step before configuring CMake |
-| `dispatch_miss_total` is above zero | Code the analysis did not find | Treat as a real bug, not noise. Each miss is a game breaking gap. Add the address to the dispatch miss seeds and regenerate. On PlayStation you read this from the debug server's `dispatch_stats` command; the cartridge toolchains write a `dispatch_misses.log` instead |
-| Compiler exits with no diagnostic, code -1 | Out of memory | Lower the parallelism: `-j2` rather than `-j"$(nproc)"` |
-| `ninja: GetLastError() = 2`, or a cache load failure | You built a directory that never configured | Fix the real configure error rather than retrying the build |
-| MinGW says `too many sections` | COFF section limit | Add `-Wa,-mbig-obj` |
-| The build zip contains the user's disc image | Packaging without the allowlist | Never hand ship a zip. Use the project's packaging script, which refuses when game data is present |
+| `Cannot find source file: generated/OpenBIOS_full.c` at configure time | The BIOS C was never generated | Run generate before configuring CMake. CMake does not fall back |
+| `dispatch_miss_total` is above zero | Code the analysis never found | Treat it as a real bug. Add the address to the seeds and regenerate. The section above says where each console reports misses |
+| Compiler exits with no message, code -1 | Out of memory | Lower the parallelism: `-j2` rather than `-j"$(nproc)"` |
+| `ninja: error: loading 'build.ninja': GetLastError() = 2`, or a cache load failure | You ran a build in a directory that never configured successfully | Fix the real configure error, then re-run the same `cmake -S ... -B ...`. Delete the build directory if a stale cache keeps poisoning the retry |
+| MinGW says `Error: too many sections` | The Windows COFF section limit | Add `-Wa,-mbig-obj` for that file. Recent binutils handle it without the flag |
+| The release zip contains the user's disc image | You zipped a directory by hand | Use the project's packaging script. It copies only the files it is told to copy, and clears `generated/` and `disc/` out of the staged tree |
 
